--TEST--
`Core\Request\Part::content` and `::readAll` both answer `tainted bytes`, so a plain `bytes` binding refuses either — the mark is on the octets and not on which way they were read — `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`, `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of the two ways § 3 gives for reaching one upload's bytes.
// What decides the qualifier is that the bytes came off a connection, and
// nothing about whether the program held them a chunk at a time or all at
// once -- so a consumer that dropped the mark would be a launderer reachable
// by choosing the other reading, which is the direction `rule:security/tainted-qualifier` does not
// trade.
//
// No runtime assertion can show it: a plain value is assignable into a
// `tainted` target, so a case that bound one of these would pass over a member
// that had lost the qualifier. The refusal is the pin, and it is the binding
// that has to fail -- `bytes` narrows through no qualifier bit.
foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
    bytes $whole = $part->readAll();
    foreach ($part->content() as bytes $chunk) {
    }
}
// covers: Core\Request\Part::content, Core\Request\Part::readAll
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `tainted bytes`
   --> case.nvs:14:20
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
